.TH LIBAST 3
.UC 4
.SH NAME
libast \- introduction to the AST library
.SH DESCRIPTION
This section describes the
.I AST
(AT&T Software Technology) library functions of the
.B libast
library.
.B libast
serves three major purposes.
First, it presents (a subset of) POSIX/ANSI standard headers and interfaces on
non-compliant systems.
Second, it provides a portable base of routines that implement concepts
common to all UNIX system variants.
Finally, it is a forum for
modern implementations of features present (or lacking)
in the standard C libraries.
Features better provided by separate libraries are omitted from
.BR libast .
For example, most terminal driver interface issues are left for
special purpose libraries such as
.IR curses (3).
.PP
The
.B libast
related header files are installed in the directories
.BR include/ast .
Routines that do not advertize their own headers are prototyped in
.BR <ast.h> .
.B <ast.h>
includes or defines the equivalent of
.BR <limits.h> ,
.BR <stddef.h> ,
.BR <stdlib.h> ,
.BR <sys/types.h> ,
.B <string.h>
and
.BR <unistd.h> .
Other libraries that depend on
.B libast
may also have headers installed in the
.B include/ast
directories.
.SS "Why libast?"
The standard C libraries provided by the *BSD and Linux systems are
deliberately minimal: they offer a common, portable baseline, but stop
well short of what non-trivial programs actually need.
.B libast
fills that gap.
Beyond merely papering over the differences between UNIX variants,
it supplies modern, carefully engineered implementations of facilities
that the standard libraries either lack entirely or provide only in
primitive form.
The components below are all part of
.B libast
and are linked with
.BR \-last ;
there is no need to track down and link a scattering of separate
libraries.
.PP
Some highlights:
.TP
.B Sfio
.RI ( sfio (3))
is a buffered I/O library that goes far beyond
.IR stdio (3).
A stream may be a file or an in-memory string, and
.I disciplines
allow application-supplied
.IR read (2)/ write (2)/ lseek (2)
analogues to be pushed onto a stream, so that filtering, compression or
transformation layers can be inserted without changing the code that
performs the I/O.
Streams may be stacked for recursive processing and gathered into pools
whose buffers are synchronized automatically.
File streams may use
.IR mmap (2)
where that is more efficient than copying through a buffer.
Even the
.BR sfprintf ()/ sfscanf ()
formatting engines are extensible: an application can define its own
conversion patterns or redefine the standard ones.
.TP
.B Cdt
.RI ( cdt (3))
manages run-time dictionaries and containers through a single, uniform
interface.
The same handle supports unordered and ordered sets and multisets,
lists, stacks, queues and deques, and the storage method can be changed
at run time with
.BR dtmethod ().
Object layout, comparison, hashing and memory management are supplied by
a separate
.I discipline,
so one discipline works unchanged with every container type.
The standard C library has nothing comparable.
.TP
.B Vmalloc
.RI ( vmalloc (3))
provides region-based memory allocation on top of
.IR malloc (3).
Each allocation belongs to a region, and an unlimited number of regions
may be opened; closing a region frees all of its memory at once.
This compartmentalizes memory management and makes leaks far easier to
avoid.
Regions can also be opened so that all allocated memory is automatically
initialized to zero, a safeguard against the use of uninitialized data.
.TP
.B Stk
.RI ( stk (3))
provides efficient stack-oriented dynamic storage for objects that are
built incrementally and released as a group, which is ideal for parsing
and code generation.
It cooperates with
.B Sfio
\(em the stack type is layout-compatible with
.BR Sfio_t ,
so the
.B Sfio
output functions can build an object directly on the stack.
.TP
.B regex
.RI ( regex (3))
is a full POSIX regular-expression library extended with shell pattern
syntax
.RB ( REG_SHELL ),
augmented operators, left/right anchoring, an LRU cache of compiled
patterns
.RB ( regcache ()),
and
.IR sed (1)-style
substitution
.RB ( regsubcomp ()/ regsubexec ()).
The companion
.BR strmatch (3)
routines match shell glob patterns directly, including the
.BR & (and)
and
.BR ! (not)
operators that ordinary regular expressions cannot express.
.TP
.B tm
.RI ( tm (3)),
.B tmx
.RI ( tmx (3))
and
.B tv
.RI ( tv (3))
handle date and time at resolutions from seconds to nanoseconds.
They not only convert and format
.BR time_t
values but parse free-form, natural-language date specifications such as
\(lqnext Tuesday noon\(rq
or
\(lq3 weeks ago\(rq
via
.BR tmdate ()/ tmxdate (),
and support ISO 8601 durations via
.BR tmxduration ().
.TP
.B optget
.RI ( optget (3))
parses command-line options and, from a single self-describing option
string, generates the command's usage message and even its
.BR \-\-man ,
.B \-\-help
and
.B \-\-html
documentation, so a command's manual and its option parsing never
drift apart.
.TP
.B error
.RI ( error (3))
provides leveled error reporting (debug, note, warning, error, fatal)
with optional internationalization through message catalogs, so a whole
family of programs can present diagnostics consistently.
.PP
In addition,
.B libast
supplies portable path manipulation
.RI ( path (3)),
file-hierarchy traversal
.RI ( fts (3)
and
.IR ftwalk (3)),
process-group-aware process creation
.RI ( spawnveg (3)),
and a large collection of string, formatting and conversion routines
.RI ( fmt (3),
.BR stropt (3),
.BR strton (3),
and many more).
.SH FILES
.nf
include/ast         the \fBast\fP package header directory
lib/libast.a        the \fBlibast\fP library
lib/libast-g.a      the \fBlibast\fP library compiled for debugging
lib/libast-pg.a     the \fBlibast\fP library compiled for profiling
lib/libast.so.4.0   the \fBlibast\fP shared library
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
intro(3),
intro(2),
cc(1)
.SH CAVEATS
The library documentation is still under construction.
Yes, some of it has been in this state since the 1980s.
Thank goodness our commands self-document.
.PP
.B libast
as a whole is currently
.B not
thread-safe.
Many of its components keep internal state in global or static storage
(for example, the
.BR tm / tmx
time-zone and format tables, the
.B regex
pattern cache, the
.B optget
and
.B error
globals, and various
.B fmt
buffers), and the library makes no attempt to serialize access to it.
It is therefore suitable for supporting classic UNIX programs that
achieve parallelism through cooperating processes \(em such as the Korn
shell \(em but
.B not
for multi-threaded applications.
(The
.BR aso (3)
atomic scalar operations are a low-level building block available to
applications that do their own synchronization; they do not make the rest
of the library thread-safe.)
